// @emit-error an i32-keyed Map/Set is supported as a binding / parameter / return / `| null` / a closure result / a map value
// CAPABILITY (B6b): the ARRAY-ELEMENT position, and the one place where the loud reject
// is DELIBERATELY KEPT rather than merely not yet done.
//
// The supported set is now a binding, a parameter, a return, `| null`, a closure RESULT
// (`() => {[i32]: V}` — the gate delegates to the return spelling) and a MAP VALUE (a
// nested `{[i32]: {[i32]: V}}`), with every VALUE type the string-keyed rep lowers.
// This position and the struct/variant FIELD are what remain.
//
// KEEPING IT IS MEASURED, NOT DEFERRED BY DEFAULT. The parity grid pairs each cell with
// its `{[string]: V}` twin: the STRING-keyed spelling of this very program — a LIST of
// maps, written through `xs[0][k] = v` — emits an INVALID MODULE today for 12 of the 14
// value types (`type mismatch: expected (ref null $type), found (ref $type)`, `vl check`
// rc 0), and only `i32` / `boolean` elements run. Opening the i32-keyed half would
// convert this CLEAN REJECT into the same invalid wasm for those 12 — a strictly worse
// outcome class than the one it replaces. The right order is to fix the string-keyed
// list-of-maps store first; the i32-keyed half then follows for free, because the
// ref-list ELEMENT row already records the whole `{[i32]: V}` spelling (unlike a field
// row) and `rlElemMapKeyI32` already reads the key off it.
const xs: {[i32]: i32}[] = [Map()]
xs[0][1] = 2
print(xs[0].length)
